import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
const content = "<p>Removing a dating app from your phone can feel like a clean ending. But the app on your device, your account with the operator and any subscription are different things. If you want to leave, the useful question is not just \u201cHave I deleted it?\u201d It is \u201cWhat has actually ended, and what information remains?\u201d</p>\n<p>To delete a dating app account and data, use the operator\u2019s account-closure process, check what its deletion policy covers and address any paid subscription through the billing provider. Account closure may start the operator\u2019s data-deletion process, but it does not necessarily mean every record disappears immediately. Keep the confirmations for each outcome separately.</p>\n<p>We are building FilWest for serious Filipino-Western relationships, and our public guides help adults make decisions around online dating. Leaving another service does not require joining ours. Whether you have met someone or simply want to stop dating online, the aim is to finish with a clear record rather than an empty space on your phone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choose-closure-rather-than-just-disappearance\">Choose Closure Rather Than Just Disappearance</h2>\n<p>Start by deciding whether you want a break or an exit. A setting described as hiding or pausing a profile may suit someone who wants to return. It should not be treated as an instruction to erase the account. Read what that particular setting changes, including whether existing conversations remain available.</p>\n<p>Tinder\u2019s help guidance makes the distinction explicit: uninstalling the Tinder app does not delete your Tinder account. Its account-deletion guidance instead directs users to sign in, open the profile icon, go to Settings and select Delete Account. It also describes a separate Manage My Account route that requires signing in and confirming deletion. Those are Tinder\u2019s instructions, not a menu path to assume on every dating service.</p>\n<p>If you have already uninstalled an app, find the operator\u2019s official account-management instructions. Removing the software is not evidence that you completed those instructions. Likewise, logging out only tells you that you have left that session; look for an explicit closure confirmation before recording the account as closed.</p>\n<p>For someone leaving because a relationship has become exclusive, there is also a conversation to have outside the settings screen. \u201cI deleted the app\u201d can mean different things to two people. Our guide to <a href=\"https://filipinawest.com/guides/long-distance-relationship-exclusivity\">agreeing on exclusivity</a> addresses that mutual agreement. Here, the task is narrower: making sure the operator receives the account action you intend.</p>\n<h2 id=\"save-what-you-need-before-closing-access\">Save What You Need Before Closing Access</h2>\n<p>Before confirming closure, consider whether you need anything from the account. Useful records might include a purchase receipt, a support reference or messages relevant to a report. Keep only what serves a purpose, and store sensitive material somewhere private rather than forwarding it casually.</p>\n<p>The order matters. Tinder says that once an account is deleted, the user loses access to download their data. If you want a copy, consult its download instructions before deleting the account. Do not assume a later support request will give you the same access you had while signed in.</p>\n<p>An export is not a deletion request. Downloading a copy gives you information to keep; it does not itself tell the operator to remove the original. It also creates another copy that you now need to manage. If your priority is reducing stored personal information, decide whether you need a full export or just a small set of records.</p>\n<p>If you are leaving after harmful conduct, consider what you need for a report before closing access. This is not a reason to remain in unwanted conversations. It is a reason to avoid losing material you meant to preserve. Personalised safety planning, particularly where someone is monitoring or threatening you, needs specialist support rather than a routine account-cleanup checklist.</p>\n<h2 id=\"read-retention-as-a-separate-question\">Read Retention As A Separate Question</h2>\n<p>A profile no longer being visible answers a question about other members\u2019 access. It does not, on its own, answer what the company still holds. <a href=\"https://policies.tinder.com/privacy/intl/en/\">Tinder\u2019s data-retention policy</a> says that after account closure your profile is no longer visible to other members in the app, while data deletion follows its retention rules.</p>\n<p>Those rules explain why a closure confirmation and an all-data-erased assurance are not interchangeable. Tinder describes a safety retention window of three months following account closure, during which it keeps data to investigate unlawful or harmful conduct. It also describes longer retention for certain limited records, including transaction information and customer-care exchanges. The safety window is therefore not a deadline after which every category of information necessarily disappears.</p>\n<p>For your own service, look for the policy section explaining what happens after closure. The useful questions are which categories remain, why the operator retains them and what period or condition governs their deletion. A statement that data is deleted \u201cin accordance with our privacy policy\u201d sends you to those details; it is not a promise of immediate erasure.</p>\n<p>Verification information deserves particular attention if you supplied it. An identity document, a video selfie and face geometry are different kinds of information. Tinder directs users to notices for particular face-data features, and its general retention wording includes exceptions. Check the notice for the feature you actually used rather than borrowing a period from another verification process or region.</p>\n<p>You do not need to turn this into a catalogue of everything an operator might collect. Concentrate on the information that matters to your departure. If you are concerned about uploaded photos, messages, location history or verification material, name those categories when asking what remains.</p>\n<h2 id=\"make-the-data-request-specific\">Make The Data Request Specific</h2>\n<p>Some operators connect account closure with their data-deletion process. That does not mean you always need two separate forms. It does mean you should understand whether the closure process addresses your concern or whether you need to contact the operator about retained information.</p>\n<p>Use the privacy contact or request route given by the service. Explain whether the account is already closed, identify the relevant account through the information the official process requires and ask for a response about deletion and retention. A useful request could say:</p>\n<p>\u201cPlease close my account if it is still open and process my request to delete the personal data associated with it. Please confirm account closure separately from the response about data deletion. If you retain any information, please explain the categories, the reason for retention and the applicable retention period or criteria. My particular concern is the verification material I submitted.\u201d</p>\n<p>Adapt the final sentence to your actual concern, or remove it. There is no benefit in claiming you supplied an identity document if you did not. Clear, accurate details give support something specific to address.</p>\n<p>An operator may need to verify that the request comes from the account holder. Tinder\u2019s policy says it may ask for information to verify identity or authority before answering a privacy request. Follow the official process, but do not volunteer passwords or send identity documents to a match who offers to help. If a request for additional information is unclear, ask what is needed and how to submit it through the operator\u2019s designated channel.</p>\n<p>Save the acknowledgement and reference number. An acknowledgement establishes that the request was received, not that erasure has finished. When the response arrives, check whether it addresses the categories you named. If it confirms only closure, follow up about the unresolved data question using the same reference.</p>\n<h2 id=\"resolve-billing-with-the-provider-that-holds-it\">Resolve Billing With The Provider That Holds It</h2>\n<p>Tinder states that deleting your account will not cancel an active subscription. Google Play likewise says that uninstalling an app will not cancel its subscription. These are concrete reasons to check billing directly rather than treating the closure screen as a payment instruction.</p>\n<p>Find the receipt or subscription record that identifies where the purchase was made. Then use that provider\u2019s cancellation process and retain its confirmation. The phone you currently use is not enough to establish who manages an earlier purchase; the purchase record is the better starting point.</p>\n<p>Check what the confirmation actually says. Stopping renewal, reaching the end of paid access and receiving a refund answer different questions. Google Play explains that after cancellation, access generally continues for the time already paid. Continued paid access therefore does not by itself establish that cancellation failed.</p>\n<p>Our guide to <a href=\"https://filipinawest.com/guides/free-vs-paid-filipina-dating\">checking subscription and billing terms</a> covers the payment decision in more detail. For leaving the account, keep the billing result alongside your closure record without treating either as proof of the other. If a charge needs investigation later, the provider\u2019s confirmation is more useful than a screenshot showing that the dating app has gone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"do-not-recreate-the-account-just-to-test-deletion\">Do Not Recreate The Account Just To Test Deletion</h2>\n<p>It can be tempting to sign up again to see whether your old information returns. That is a poor way to check the outcome when the operator supports restoration.</p>\n<p>Tinder\u2019s policy says that creating a new account with the same credentials within 90 days will restore some data. Its deletion help describes the restore feature as being tested in select markets. That is a service-specific reason to read the restoration wording before creating another account, not a rule that every dating platform behaves this way.</p>\n<p>Use the closure confirmation and the operator\u2019s response to your data request as your records. If they appear inconsistent, ask the operator to clarify the account\u2019s status. Creating another profile can introduce a new account action when you are trying to establish what happened to the old one.</p>\n<p>Also keep the scope of your request realistic. Tinder\u2019s policy says another member requesting their own data may receive messages you sent them. A request to the operator should not be treated as confirmation that every copy held by another person has disappeared. Ask about the information under the operator\u2019s control rather than assuming closure can recall everything previously shared.</p>\n<h2 id=\"leaving-our-launch-list-is-not-deleting-a-dating-profile\">Leaving Our Launch List Is Not Deleting A Dating Profile</h2>\n<p>Our current position is different from an operating dating service. FilWest is a prelaunch project with public educational guides and an opt-in launch email list. Joining that list does not create a dating profile. We are not offering a current public dating subscription or providing account-deletion instructions for a released dating product here.</p>\n<p>If you joined our launch list, you can withdraw consent or request deletion by emailing support@filipinawest.com. Identify the email address used for the list and make clear whether you want to withdraw from updates, request deletion of the entry, or both.</p>\n<p>Launch-list consent is separate from any future account or wider marketing consent. We retain the launch-list entry until you withdraw consent or we no longer need it for launch and access updates. A response about leaving that list concerns the list entry, not the deletion of matches, messages or a dating profile.</p>\n<h2 id=\"finish-with-separate-confirmation-records\">Finish With Separate Confirmation Records</h2>\n<p>A complete exit is not one reassuring screen. Keep an account-closure confirmation, the acknowledgement and outcome of any data-deletion request, and the billing provider\u2019s cancellation confirmation if you had a paid plan. For retained data, keep the explanation of what remains and the period or condition that applies.</p>\n<p>These records let you follow up on the part that is unresolved. A closed account with an unanswered privacy request needs a data response. A cancelled subscription with an open profile still needs account closure. Neither situation is fixed by uninstalling again.</p>\n<p>The practical next step is to find your operator\u2019s closure instructions and privacy-request route while you still have access, save any records you need, and deal with billing through the provider named on the receipt. If your only connection with us is the FilWest launch list, email our support address about that entry. You can leave without creating another account, and without mistaking one kind of confirmation for another.</p>";
